= Turing completeness =

== Overview ==
'''Turing completeness''' is the property of a computational system being able, in principle, to simulate a universal Turing machine under idealized unbounded resources.

A Turing-complete programming language or machine model can express any Turing-computable computation.

== Expressive power ==
Turing completeness concerns what computations can be expressed, not how conveniently or efficiently they run.

Very small, strange, or impractical systems can be Turing complete.

Useful restricted systems may deliberately not be Turing complete.

== Resource assumptions ==
Turing-completeness claims normally assume unbounded memory or equivalent idealized resources.

A real physical computer has finite memory and is therefore not literally an unbounded Turing machine.

The concept classifies the computational model rather than the finite device.

== Universality and simulation ==
A Turing-complete system can encode and simulate arbitrary Turing machines.

This makes universal interpretation possible.

== Turing completeness and decidability ==
Turing completeness does not mean every question about programs is decidable.

On the contrary, Turing-complete systems inherit undecidable program-analysis problems such as general halting.

== Turing completeness and complexity ==
Two systems can be Turing complete while differing dramatically in efficiency.

Therefore:

<pre>
Turing complete ≠ fast
Turing complete ≠ practical
</pre>

== Terminology note ==
Computability theory also uses “Turing complete” for certain sets/degrees under Turing reducibility.

This article concerns the common computational-system sense.

